erated futures to physically meaningful execution.\nThis imagination paradigm also scales naturally toward synthetic data generation and high-level planning. When future generation is conditioned on task or language descriptions, the resulting videos can serve as high-level demonstration surrogates or task-relevant synthetic supervision. They can also function as visual plans for long-horizon decision making (Du et al.,2024;Chen et al.,2025a). DreamGen (Jang et al., 2025a) adapts strong video generators to a target embodiment, synthesizes neural trajectories, and recovers executable actions through latent action modeling or inverse dynamics. Its central message is that stronger video world models can be used not only to regularize policies, but also to produce synthetic experience that improves downstream generalization. Taken together, these works establish the first major role of robotic video generation, namely to serve as an imagination engine that broadens the supervisory and planning signals available for policy learning.\n5.3Toward Action-Controllable Video World Models\nA second class of methods shifts the emphasis from imagined supervision to explicit controllability. Here the central question is no longer whether a model can produce plausible future videos, but whether the generated future follows the commanded action sequence with sufficient precision to support manipulation reasoning and downstream control. This shift is important because, in embodied settings, a visually convincing rollout is of limited value if it does not respond faithfully to action intervention.\nIRASim (Zhu et al.,2025b) is representative of this transition. It formulates robot manipulation as a trajectory-to-video problem and introduces frame-level action conditioning within each transformer block to strengthen alignment between individual actions and corresponding future frames. RoboEnvision (Yang et al.,2025) focuses on long-horizon multi-task manipulation and emphasizes the difficulty of preserving semantic and temporal consistency over extended task evolution. RoboMaster (Fu et al.,2026) addresses more complex robot-object interactions through collaborative trajectory control. By decomposing manipulation into multiple phases and modeling the coupled motion of the robot arm and the manipulated object, it improves faithfulness under rich contact dynamics. Ctrl-World (Guo et al.,2026b) pushes controllability further toward policy-in-the-loop rollout. It combines joint multi-view prediction, frame-level action control, and memory-based long-horizon generation so that predicted futures can support both policy evaluation and targeted policy improvement. EnerVerse-AC (Jiang et al.,2025c) follows a related direction and formulates the world model as an action-conditional multi-view generator that can act both as a data engine and as an evaluator for robotic inference. Interactive World Simulator (Wang et al.,2026c) pushes this line from controllable generation toward genuinely interactive simulation, emphasizing high-frequency, long-horizon, and stable policy-conditioned interaction for closed-loop rollout, demonstration collection, and policy evaluation. EVA (Wang et al.,2026b) complements this direction from the perspective of post-training alignment by targeting the executability gap between visually plausible rollouts and physically executable robot behavior, using inverse-dynamics rewards to align video world models with smooth, embodiment-consistent action sequences.\nCollectively, these works mark a decisive conceptual shift. For robotic video world models, fidelity is increasingly measured not only by realism, but also by action faithfulness, controllable interaction, and usefulness for closed-loop decision making.\n5.4Structure-Aware Generation with Interaction and Geometry Priors\nA closely related thread improves controllability by introducing richer intermediate structure for interaction. Instead of conditioning only on low-dimensional action sequences, these methods encode masks, geometry, viewpoints, or identity cues that better preserve contact relations and scene structure. The underlying intuition is that robotic video generation becomes substantially more useful when the model is required to preserve explicit interaction structure, rather than merely synthesize visually convincing motion.\nMask2IV (Li et al.,2025a) is illustrative of this idea. It adopts a two-stage design that first predicts interaction trajectories for the actor and the object, and then generates a video conditioned on these trajectories. This removes the need for dense user-provided masks while retaining flexible control over interaction outcomes.\n21\n运动落地为机器人动作,弥合了照片级真实运动与物理可执行行为之间的差距。这些方法超越了纯粹的视觉想象, 开始将生成的未来与物理上有意义的执行连接起来。 这一想象范式同样能够自然地扩展至合成数据生成与高层规划。当未来生成以任务或语言描述为条件时,所 生成的视频可作为高层示范的替代物或任务相关的合成监督信号。它们还可以充当长时程决策的视觉规划 (Du et al., 2024; Chen et al., 2025a)。DreamGen(Jang et al., 2025a)将强大的视频生成器适配 至目标具身形态,合成神经轨迹,并通过潜在动作建模或逆动力学恢复可执行动作。其核心信息在于,更强 大的视频世界模型不仅可用于正则化策略,还可用于生成合成经验以提升下游泛化能力。综合来看,这些工 作确立了机器人视频生成的第一个主要角色,即作为想象引擎,拓宽策略学习可用的监督信号与规划信号。\n5.3 走向动作可控的视频世界模型\n第二类方法将重心从想象监督转向显式可控性。此时核心问题不再是模型能否生成看似合理的未来视频,而 是所生成的未来是否以足够精度遵循指令动作序列,从而支持操作推理与下游控制。这一转变至关重要,因 为在具身场景中,若视觉上令人信服的推演不能忠实响应动作干预,其价值便十分有限。\nIRASim(Zhu 等,2025b)是这一转变的代表性工作。它将机器人操作建模为轨迹到视频的问题,并在每 个 transformer 块中引入帧级动作条件,以增强单个动作与对应未来帧之间的对齐。RoboEnvision( Yang 等,2025)聚焦于长时程多任务操作,并强调在任务长程演化中保持语义与时间一致性的难度。 RoboMaster(Fu 等,2026)通过协同轨迹控制来处理更复杂的机器人‐物体交互。它将操作分解为多个阶 段,并对机械臂与被操作物体的耦合运动进行建模,从而在丰富接触动力学下提升保真度。Ctrl‐World( Guo 等,2026b)将可控性进一步推向策略在环的 rollout。它结合了联合多视图预测、帧级动作控制和基 于记忆的长时程生成,使预测的未来能够同时支持策略评估和有针对性的策略改进。EnerVerse‐AC( Jiang 等,2025c)遵循相关方向,将世界模型表述为动作条件多视图生成器,既可作为数据引擎,也可作 为机器人推理的评估器。Interactive World Simulator(Wang 等,2026c)将这一路线从可控生成推向真 正交互式的仿真,强调高频、长时程且稳定的策略条件交互,以支持闭环 rollout、演示收集和策略评估。 EVA(Wang 等,2026b)从训练后对齐的角度补充了这一方向,针对视觉上合理的 rollout 与物理上可执 行的机器人行为之间的可执行性差距,利用逆动力学奖励将视频世界模型与平滑、具身一致的动作序列对齐。\n总体而言,这些工作标志着一次决定性的概念转变。对于机器人视频世界模型而言,保真度日益不仅以真实 感来衡量,还以动作忠实性、可控交互性以及对闭环决策的实用性来衡量。\n5.4 融合交互与几何先验的结构感知生成\n一个密切相关的方向通过引入更丰富的交互中间结构来提升可控性。这些方法不再仅以低维动作序列为条件, 而是编码掩码、几何、视角或身份线索,从而更好地保持接触关系和场景结构。其基本直觉是:当模型被要 求保持显式的交互结构,而非仅仅合成视觉上令人信服的运动时,机器人视频生成会变得显著更有用。\nMask2IV(Li 等,2025a)很好地说明了这一思路。它采用两阶段设计,首先预测执行者和物体的交互轨迹, 然后以这些轨迹为条件生成视频。这消除了对用户提供密集掩码的需求,同时保留了对交互结果的灵活控制。\n21\n\n\n TesserAct (Zhen et al.,2025) pushes structure further by extending the representation space from 2D video to a 4D embodied world model over RGB, depth, and normal signals, improving spatial consistency and enabling stronger inverse dynamics and policy learning. RoboVIP (Wang et al.,2026a) focuses on a practical requirement of modern policy learners, namely temporally coherent multi-view observations. It introduces visual identity prompting to guide multi-view video diffusion and uses the resulting videos as scalable augmentation for manipulation data.\nThis structure-aware view also connects robotic video world models to a broader line of structured and symbolic world modeling. While the methods above preserve structure inside generated visual futures, another family abstracts the world into predicates, object relations, affordances, or causal processes and predicts their transitions for planning (Liang et al.,2025c;Athalye et al.,2026;Liang et al.,2026). These approaches do not aim to improve visual fidelity; instead, they target more compact and compositional predictive variables that may be better aligned with long-horizon reasoning and executable control.\nAlthough these methods differ in representation and objective, they share a common principle: richer structural priors can make generated futures more controllable, more consistent across views and contacts, and ultimately more useful for downstream embodied learning.\n5.5From Video Backbones to Foundation World Models\nThe most recent works reinterpret robotic video world models as general-purpose interactive predictors built by adapting large-scale video backbones. In this regime, video generation is no longer merely a downstream augmentation tool. It becomes a reusable substrate for simulation, planning, evaluation, and large-scale robot data production.\nVid2World (Huang et al.,2026) is a canonical example of this shift. Rather than training a robotic world model from scratch, it systematically transforms a pretrained video diffusion model into an interactive world model suitable for action-conditioned rollout. Genie Envisioner (Liao et al.,2026) extends this idea into a unified world foundation platform that integrates video world modeling with action decoding for robotic manipulation. DreamDojo (Gao et al.,2026a) pushes the foundation-model regime further by pretraining on large-scale human egocentric videos, introducing continuous latent actions to bridge unlabeled human interaction and robot control, and then post-training the model for target embodiments. It shows that such a model can support long-horizon real-time rollout, policy evaluation, and model-based planning.\nA complementary argument is made by WoW (Chi et al.,2025c). It emphasizes that physical intuition cannot be acquired from passive video observation alone, and instead trains a large generative world model on extensive robot interaction trajectories. By coupling generative rollout with inverse dynamics and critique, it explicitly closes the imagination-to-action loop. At the platform level, UnifoLM-WMA-0 (Unitree,2025) and Cosmos Predict 2.5 (Ali et al.,2025) further reflect the trend toward reusable world backbones, while GigaWorld-0 (Team et al.,2025b) makes the data-engine perspective fully explicit by combining a controllable video branch with a physically grounded 3D branch for large-scale embodied data synthesis. ABot-PhysWorld (Chen et al.,2026d) extends this trend toward physics-aligned world foundation models. It explicitly targets physically plausible and action-controllable manipulation video generation.\nTaken together, these works indicate a broader transition in the field. Robotic video generation is increasingly treated not as an isolated generative task, but as a foundation layer for interactive world modeling.\n5.6Technical Progression and Open Challenges\nViewed together, the literature reveals a clear technical progression. Early methods such as Dreamitate, RoboDreamer, DreMa, ManipDreamer, DreamGen, and PhysWorld mainly treat video generation as imagination that supplies additional supervision for policy learning (Liang et al.,2024;Zhou et al.,2024;Barcellona et al., 2025;Li et al.,2025f;Jang et al.,2025a;Mao et al.,2025). The next wave, including IRASim, RoboEnvision, RoboMaster, Ctrl-World, EnerVerse-AC, Interactive World Simulator, and EVA, makes action alignment, controllable rollout, interactive usability, executability, and evaluation utility central objectives (Zhu et al., 2025b;Yang et al.,2025;Fu et al.,2026;Guo et al.,2026b;Jiang et al.,2025c;Wang et al.,2026c,b). A parallel line introduces richer interaction structure through masks, geometry, and multi-view identity cues, as\n22\nTesserAct(Zhen 等,2025)进一步推进了结构建模,将表示空间从二维视频扩展到基于 RGB、深度和法 向信号的 4D 具身世界模型,提升了空间一致性,并实现了更强的逆动力学和策略学习。RoboVIP( Wang 等,2026a)关注现代策略学习者的一个实际需求,即时间一致的多视角观测。它引入视觉身份提示 来引导多视角视频扩散,并将生成的视频用作操作数据的可扩展增强。\n这种结构感知视角还将机器人视频世界模型与更广泛的结构化与符号化世界建模研究联系起来。虽然上述方 法在生成的视觉未来中保留了结构,但另一类方法将世界抽象为谓词、物体关系、可供性或因果过程,并预 测它们的状态转移以用于规划(Liang et al., 2025c; Athalye et al., 2026; Liang et al., 2026)。这些 方法并不旨在提升视觉保真度;相反,它们追求更紧凑、更具组合性的预测变量,这些变量可能更契合长时 程推理与可执行控制。\n尽管这些方法在表示和目标上各不相同,但它们共享一个共同原则:更丰富的结构先验能使生成的未来更可 控、在不同视角与接触条件下更一致,并最终对下游具身学习更有用。\n5.5 从视频骨干网络到基础世界模型\n最近的工作将机器人视频世界模型重新诠释为通过适配大规模视频骨干网络构建的通用交互式预测器。在这 一范式下,视频生成不再仅仅是下游的增强工具,而是成为用于仿真、规划、评估和大规模机器人数据生产 的可复用基础。\nVid2World(Huang 等,2026)是这一转变的典型范例。它并非从零开始训练机器人世界模型,而是系统 性地将预训练视频扩散模型转化为适用于动作条件推演的交互式世界模型。Genie Envisioner(Liao 等, 2026)将这一思路扩展为统一的世界基础平台,将视频世界建模与动作解码相集成以服务于机器人操作。 DreamDojo(Gao 等,2026a)进一步推进了基础模型范式,在大规模人类自我中心视频上进行预训练, 引入连续潜在动作以弥合无标注人类交互与机器人控制之间的鸿沟,随后针对目标具身进行后训练。该工作 表明,此类模型能够支持长时程实时推演、策略评估以及基于模型的规划。\nWoW(Chi 等,2025c)提出了一个互补性论点。它强调物理直觉无法仅通过被动视频观察获得,转而基于 大量机器人交互轨迹训练大规模生成式世界模型。通过将生成式推演与逆动力学和评判机制相耦合,它显式 地闭合了从想象到行动的环路。在平台层面,UnifoLM‐WMA‐0(Unitree,2025)和 Cosmos Predict 2.5(Ali 等,2025)进一步反映了可复用世界骨干网络的发展趋势,而 GigaWorld‐0(Team 等,2025b) 通过将可控视频分支与具有物理基础的 3D 分支相结合以支持大规模具身数据合成,使数据引擎视角得到了 充分明确的体现。ABot‐PhysWorld(Chen 等,2026d)将这一趋势延伸至物理对齐的世界基础模型。它 明确面向物理上合理且动作可控的操作视频生成。\n综合来看,这些工作表明该领域正在经历更广泛的转变。机器人视频生成越来越不被视为一项孤立的生成任务, 而是被视为交互式世界建模的基础层。\n5.6 技术进展与开放挑战\n综合来看,这些文献呈现出清晰的技术演进脉络。早期方法如 Dreamitate、Robo‐Dreamer、DreMa、 ManipDreamer、DreamGen 和 PhysWorld 主要将视频生成视为想象,为策略学习提供额外监督(Liang et al., 2024; Zhou et al., 2024; Barcellona et al., 2025; Li et al., 2025f; Jang et al., 2025a; Mao et al., 2025)。下一波研究,包括 IRASim、RoboEnvision、RoboMaster、Ctrl‐World、EnerVerse‐AC、I nteractive World Simulator 和 EVA,将动作对齐、可控推演、交互可用性、可执行性和评估效用作为核心目标 (Zhu et al., 2025b; Yang et al., 2025; Fu et al., 2026; Guo et al., 2026b; Jiang et al., 2025c; Wang et al., 2026c,b)。一条并行的研究路线通过掩码、几何和多视角身份线索引入更丰富的交互结构,如\n22\n\n\n in Mask2IV, TesserAct, and RoboVIP (Li et al.,2025a;Zhen et al.,2025;Wang et al.,2026a). The newest systems, including Vid2World, Genie Envisioner, DreamDojo, WoW, ABot-PhysWorld, UnifoLM-WMA-0, Cosmos Predict 2.5, and GigaWorld-0, increasingly elevate robotic video generation into a reusable foundation layer for embodied world modeling (Huang et al.,2026;Liao et al.,2026;Gao et al.,2026a;Chi et al.,2025c; Chen et al.,2026d;Unitree,2025;Ali et al.,2025;Team et al.,2025b).\nThis progression also clarifies the central bottleneck of the field. The key challenge is no longer simply to generate realistic futures. It is to generate futures that remain causally aligned with robot actions, physically and kinematically self-consistent over long horizons, coherent across views and embodiments, stable under interaction, and executable enough to support real policy improvement. For robotics, therefore, the true value of video generation lies in turning future prediction into a controllable, interactive, and actionable interface between perception and decision making.\n6World Model for Other Applications\n6.1World Model for Navigation\nWorld models have become a useful abstraction for embodied navigation, where agents must act under severe partial observability and reason about spaces, objects, or paths that are not yet visible. Instead of treating navigation as a purely reactive next-step decision problem, this line of work uses world models to imagine action-conditioned future observations, construct future-aware planning states, or derive value-like signals from imagined trajectories before the agent physically moves (Koh et al.,2021;Bar et al.,2025;Huang et al., 2025b;Nie et al.,2025;Sharma et al.). In this sense, the world model turns unseen space into a predictive planning substrate for reasoning about future visibility, traversability, and goal progress (Koh et al.,2021; Bar et al.,2025).\nEarly works emphasize look-ahead prediction of unseen observations. Pathdreamer generates plausible future 360◦ RGB, depth, and semantic observations for unvisited indoor viewpoints, showing that planning with imagined observations can substantially reduce the gap to planning with true future observations (Koh et al., 2021). VISTA introduces an “imagine-and-align” strategy for instruction-conditioned visual imagination (Huang et al.,2025c), while VISTAv2 extends this into a navigation world model that rolls out egocentric futures under candidate actions and projects them into an online value map for planning (Huang et al., 2025b). A parallel trend scales this idea into controllable video predictors: NWM formulates controllable video generation explicitly as a navigation world model (Bar et al.,2025); SparseVideoNav replaces dense long-horizon rollout with sparse future generation for faster deployment (Zhang et al.,2026); and EgoWM adapts pretrained Internet-scale video diffusion models into action-conditioned egocentric world models through lightweight conditioning (Bagchi et al.,2026). Taken together, these methods show that the value of world models in navigation lies less in visual realism itself than in exposing hidden future structure in a form usable for planning, trajectory ranking, and value estimation.\n6.2World Model for Autonomous Driving\nWorld models have likewise become an important paradigm in autonomous driving, where they increasingly unify perception, prediction, planning, and simulation. Compared with robotic manipulation, driving places stronger demands on long-horizon forecasting, multi-agent interaction, structured geometry, and safety-critical planning. Accordingly, driving world models often learn future-evolving scene representations—in image space, multi-view space, occupancy space, or compact latent space—and use them to support downstream planning or end-to-end driving decisions (Hu et al.,2022,2023;Zheng et al.,2024;Wang et al.,2024b). Early and representative works already reveal two complementary routes. One emphasizes compact or structured predictive states: MILE learns a latent dynamics model with geometric inductive bias for urban driving (Hu et al.,2022), while OccWorld formulates world modeling in 3D occupancy space so that ego motion and scene evolution are represented in a planning-compatible form (Zheng et al.,2024). The other makes the generative world-model view more explicit: GAIA-1 casts driving world modeling as multimodal sequence modeling over video, text, and action tokens (Hu et al.,2023), and DriveDreamer uses diffusion-based modeling with structural constraints to capture complex traffic evolution from real driving data (Wang et al.,2024a).\n23\nMask2IV、TesserAct 和 RoboVIP 中所见(Li et al., 2025a; Zhen et al., 2025; Wang et al., 2026a)。最新的系统,包 括 Vid2World、Genie Envisioner、DreamDojo、WoW、ABot‐PhysWorld、UnifoLM‐WMA‐0、Cosmos Predict 2.5 和 GigaWorld‐0,日益将机器人视频生成提升为具身世界建模的可复用基础层(Huang et al., 2026; Liao et al., 2026; Gao et al., 2026a; Chi et al., 2025c; Chen et al., 2026d; Unitree, 2025; Ali et al., 2025; Team et al., 2025b)。\n这一演进过程也阐明了该领域的核心瓶颈。关键挑战不再仅仅是生成逼真的未来。而是要生成与机器人动作 保持因果一致、在长时程上物理与运动学自洽、跨视角与跨具身保持连贯、在交互下保持稳定,并且足够可 执行以支撑真实策略改进的未来。因此,对于机器人学而言,视频生成的真正价值在于将未来预测转化为感 知与决策之间可控、可交互且可操作的接口。\n6 面向其他应用的世界模型\n6.1 面向导航的世界模型\n世界模型已成为具身导航中一种有用的抽象表示,在该场景中,智能体必须在严重部分可观测的条件下行动, 并对尚未可见的空间、物体或路径进行推理。这一研究方向不再将导航视为纯粹反应式的下一步决策问题, 而是利用世界模型来想象以动作为条件的未来观测、构建面向未来的规划状态,或在智能体实际移动之前从 想象轨迹中导出类价值信号(Koh et al., 2021; Bar et al., 2025; Huang et al., 2025b; Nie et al., 2025; Sharma et al.)。在此意义上,世界模型将未见的空间转化为一种预测性规划基底,用于推理未来 的可见性、可通行性以及目标进展(Koh et al., 2021; Bar et al., 2025)。\n早期工作强调对未见观测的前瞻性预测。Pathdreamer为未访问的室内视角生成合理的未来360◦ RGB、深 度和语义观测,表明使用想象观测进行规划可以大幅缩小与使用真实未来观测进行规划之间的差距(Koh et al., 2021)。VISTA引入了一种“想象并对齐”策略用于指令条件化的视觉想象(Huang et al., 2025c), 而VISTAv2将其扩展为导航世界模型,在候选动作下推演自我中心未来,并将其投影到在线价值图上用于规 划(Huang et al., 2025b)。一个并行的趋势将这一思想扩展为可控视频预测器:NWM明确将可控视频生 成表述为导航世界模型(Bar et al., 2025);SparseVideoNav用稀疏未来生成替代稠密长时程推演以实现 更快的部署(Zhang et al., 2026);EgoWM通过轻量级条件化将预训练的互联网规模视频扩散模型适配 为动作条件化的自我中心世界模型(Bagchi et al., 2026)。综合来看,这些方法表明世界模型在导航中的 价值与其说在于视觉真实感本身,不如说在于以可用于规划、轨迹排序和价值估计的形式揭示隐藏的未来结 构。\n6.2 面向自动驾驶的世界模型\n世界模型同样已成为自动驾驶领域的重要范式,在该领域中日益统一了感知、预测、规划与仿真。与机器人 操作相比,驾驶对长时域预测、多智能体交互、结构化几何以及安全关键规划提出了更高的要求。相应地, 驾驶世界模型通常学习未来演化的场景表示——在图像空间、多视图空间、占据空间或紧凑潜空间中——并 利用这些表示来支持下游规划或端到端驾驶决策(Hu et al., 2022, 2023; Zheng et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2024b)。早期具有代表性的工作已经揭示了两条互补的路径。一条强调紧凑或结构化的预测状态: MILE 学习了一个具有几何归纳偏置的潜动力学模型用于城市驾驶(Hu et al., 2022),而 OccWorld 在 3D 占据空间中构建世界建模,使自车运动与场景演化以规划兼容的形式被表示(Zheng et al., 2024)。 另一条则更明确地采用生成式世界模型视角:GAIA‐1 将驾驶世界建模视为对视频、文本和动作 token 的多 模态序列建模(Hu et al., 2023),DriveDreamer 则使用带有结构约束的扩散建模来从真实驾驶数据中捕 捉复杂的交通演化(Wang et al., 2024a)。\n23\n\n\n More recent work pushes these directions toward planning-oriented and unified driving intelligence. DriveWM generates controllable multiview future videos and uses imagined multi-future rollouts together with image-based rewards to select safer trajectories (Wang et al.,2024b). UniDWM further argues for a structureand dynamics-aware latent world representation as a unified substrate for perception, prediction, and planning (Xiong et al.,2026). DriveWorld-VLA strengthens the connection between world modeling and action generation by using latent world states as the planner’s decision state, allowing action-conditioned imagination to guide control without expensive pixel rollout (Liu et al.,2026a). From the scaling perspective, DriveVLA-W0 shows that future-image prediction through world modeling provides dense self-supervision that improves end-to-end driving VLAs beyond low-dimensional action supervision alone (Li et al.,2026c). Furthering this hierarchical synergy, SteerVLA (Gao et al.,2026b) conceptually frames the high-level VLM as a semantic world model that generates fine-grained, common-sense reasoning to steer a low-level VLA policy through complex, long-tail driving maneuvers. Overall, world models in autonomous driving are best understood as a bridge from passive scene understanding to predictive driving intelligence (Hu et al.,2023;Wang et al.,2024b; Xiong et al.,2026): some methods use them as controllable simulators of future scenes, some as structured state spaces for planning, and some as dense predictive supervision for scaling end-to-end driving policies (Zheng et al.,2024;Li et al.,2026c). Across these variants, the shared intuition is the same: safe driving requires not only recognizing the current scene, but reasoning over how it may evolve under ego behavior and surrounding traffic dynamics (Hu et al.,2022;Wang et al.,2024a;Xiong et al.,2026).\n7Benchmarks, Datasets, and Results\n7.1Benchmarks for World Model Evaluation\nIn embodied intelligence, evaluating world models differs fundamentally from evaluating video generation models in conventional computer vision. In robotics, the value of a world model depends on whether it can generate action-conditioned future states that remain consistent with real physical dynamics. This requires the model to capture robot–environment interactions beyond surface-level realism, functioning instead as a faithful predictor of physically plausible and temporally coherent future observations.\nMore importantly, such a model should respond reliably to action interventions, remain coherent over long horizons, and support downstream tasks such as policy learning, planning, and evaluation. From this policycentric perspective, visual realism alone is neither necessary nor sufficient (Shang et al.,2026), since rollouts may look convincing while still violating dynamics in ways that break closed-loop control (Qin et al.,2025;Li et al.,2025e). Accordingly, we organize existing embodied world model benchmarks into three complementary categories: (i) action-conditioned generation and open-loop predictive quality, (ii) closed-loop task utility and policy evaluation, and (iii) physical consistency, controllability, and executability diagnostics.\n7.1.1Action-conditioned generation and open-loop predictive quality\nThe first dimension evaluates embodied world models in an open-loop setting. Given the current observation together with an action sequence, language instruction, or task specification, the model is asked to autoregressively generate future observations without being embedded into a planner or control loop. The key question is whether the predicted future remains faithful to the commanded behavior over time, in terms of semantic correctness, temporal coherence, and action responsiveness, rather than visual plausibility alone. Open-loop benchmarks are attractive because they are relatively easy to scale and standardize, although their results should be interpreted with care.\nRecent benchmarks in this direction have become increasingly embodied. Rather than treating robot video generation as generic video synthesis, RBench (Deng et al.,2026) and EWMBench (Yue et al.,2025) evaluate whether generated futures preserve the task-relevant structure of embodied interaction. RBench emphasizes structural consistency, physical plausibility, and action completeness across diverse robotic tasks and embodiments. EWMBench adopts a more factorized view, separating scene consistency, motion correctness, and semantic alignment. Together, they reflect a broader shift in open-loop evaluation from appearance-level realism to interaction-faithful prediction.\nOther benchmarks further connect open-loop prediction to downstream utility. DreamGen Bench (Jang\n24\n更近期的工作将这些方向推向面向规划的统一驾驶智能。Drive‐WM 生成可控的多视图未来视频,并利用想 象的多未来推演结合基于图像的奖励来选择更安全的轨迹(Wang et al., 2024b)。UniDWM 进一步主张 以结构和动力学感知的潜世界表示作为感知、预测和规划的统一基座(Xiong et al., 2026)。 DriveWorld‐VLA 通过将潜世界状态用作规划器的决策状态,增强了世界建模与动作生成之间的联系,使动 作条件下的想象能够在无需昂贵像素推演的情况下引导控制(Liu et al., 2026a)。从缩放的角度来看, DriveVLA‐W0 表明通过世界建模进行未来图像预测提供了密集的自监督,能够超越仅依赖低维动作监督来 改进端到端驾驶 VLA(Li et al., 2026c)。为进一步推进这种分层协同,SteerVLA(Gao et al., 2026b) 在概念上将高层 VLM 构建为语义世界模型,生成细粒度的常识推理以引导底层 VLA 策略完成复杂的长尾驾 驶操作。总体而言,自动驾驶中的世界模型最好被理解为从被动场景理解到预测性驾驶智能的桥梁(Hu et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2024b; Xiong et al., 2026):一些方法将其用作未来场景的可控模拟器,一些将 其用作规划的结构化状态空间,还有一些将其用作缩放端到端驾驶策略的密集预测监督(Zheng et al., 2024; Li et al., 2026c)。在这些变体之间,共享的直觉是相同的:安全驾驶不仅需要识别当前场景,还需 要推理其在自车行为和周围交通动力学下可能如何演化(Hu et al., 2022; Wang et al., 2024a; Xiong et al., 2026)。\n7 基准测试、数据集与结果\n7.1 世界模型评估基准\n在具身智能中,评估世界模型与在传统计算机视觉中评估视频生成模型有着根本性的不同。在机器人领域, 世界模型的价值取决于它能否生成与真实物理动力学保持一致的动作条件未来状态。这要求模型不仅具有表 面上的真实感,还要捕捉机器人—环境交互,从而作为物理上合理且时间上连贯的未来观测的忠实预测器。\n更重要的是,这样的模型应当对动作干预做出可靠响应,在长时程上保持连贯,并支持策略学习、规划与评 估等下游任务。从这种以策略为中心的视角来看,仅凭视觉真实感既不必要也不充分(Shang et al., 2026),因为推演可能看起来令人信服,却仍以破坏闭环控制的方式违反动力学(Qin et al., 2025; Li et al., 2025e)。据此,我们将现有的具身世界模型基准组织为三个互补类别:(i) 动作条件生成与开环预测质 量,(ii) 闭环任务效用与策略评估,以及 (iii) 物理一致性、可控性与可执行性诊断。\n7.1.1 动作条件生成与开环预测质量\n第一个维度在开环设置下评估具身世界模型。给定当前观测以及动作序列、语言指令或任务规范,模型被要 求自回归地生成未来观测,而不被嵌入到规划器或控制回路中。关键问题在于,预测的未来是否在语义正确 性、时间连贯性和动作响应性方面随时间保持对所命令行为的忠实,而不仅仅是视觉上的合理性。开环基准 具有吸引力,因为它们相对容易扩展和标准化,尽管其结果应谨慎解读。\n该方向上近期的基准越来越具身化。RBench(Deng et al., 2026)和EWMBench(Yue et al., 2025)不 再将机器人视频生成视为通用视频合成,而是评估生成的未来是否保留了具身交互中与任务相关的结构。 RBench强调跨多样化机器人任务和具身形态的结构一致性、物理合理性和动作完整性。EWMBench采用更 为因子化的视角,将场景一致性、运动正确性和语义对齐分离。二者共同反映了开环评估从外观层面的真实 感向交互忠实预测的更广泛转变。\n其他基准测试 f 进一步将开环预测与下游效用联系起来。DreamGen Bench(Jan g\n24\n\n\n et al.,2025a) evaluates instruction following and physics alignment, asking whether generated rollouts are useful as synthetic experience for policy learning rather than merely realistic. EVA-Bench (Chi et al.,2025b) complements this view by emphasizing long-horizon anticipation and out-of-domain robustness under variations in viewpoint, scene layout, and motion distribution. Overall, these benchmarks suggest that strong open-loop world models must do more than generate plausible futures: they must remain action-grounded, physically sensible, and robust enough to support downstream embodied use.\n7.1.2Closed-loop task utility and policy evaluation\nWhile open-loop benchmarks evaluate whether a world model can generate action-conditioned futures, closedloop benchmarks ask whether those predictions remain useful inside an interactive decision loop. In this setting, the world model is evaluated not as a passive predictor, but as an environment simulator, policy evaluator, or planning substrate that directly influences action selection over time. The focus therefore shifts from predictive plausibility to decision utility: whether the model preserves the task-relevant dynamics needed for policy ranking, value estimation, planning, and ultimately task success. This makes closed-loop evaluation more aligned with embodied intelligence, since small modeling errors can accumulate and break control once the agent repeatedly acts on model-generated futures.\nRecent benchmarks in this category differ in protocol, but share the same principle: a useful embodied world model must support downstream decision making, not just realistic generation. WorldArena (Shang et al., 2026) makes this explicit by evaluating world models not only with perceptual criteria, but also through functional roles such as synthetic data generation, policy evaluation, and action planning, highlighting the gap between visual realism and embodied utility. WorldEval (Li et al.,2025e) operationalizes this idea through comparative policy assessment, asking whether rollouts in a learned world model preserve the relative ordering of robot policies and checkpoints. WorldGym (Quevedo et al.,2025) extends this setting by treating the learned model as an interactive environment for Monte Carlo evaluation, focusing on whether estimated policy values and success trends match those in the real world. Across these works, rank consistency, value fidelity, and decision reliability emerge as more informative criteria than pixel-level accuracy.\nA stricter version of this evaluation places the world model directly inside a closed-loop planning pipeline and measures embodied task success. World-in-World (Zhang et al.,2025a) is representative of this setting: by providing a unified interface for integrating heterogeneous world models into online planning tasks, it tests whether the model can improve control under iterative prediction and replanning. This is a harder setting than open-loop rollout evaluation because it exposes compounding errors that arise when prediction and action interact over time. Overall, recent evidence suggests that visual plausibility is only a weak proxy for control utility, whereas action-grounded consistency and controllability are much more reliable indicators of downstream embodied usefulness.\n7.1.3Physical consistency, controllability, and executability diagnostics\nWhile open-loop benchmarks evaluate predictive quality and closed-loop benchmarks evaluate downstream utility, diagnostic benchmarks ask a more targeted question: which properties of a generated rollout determine whether it is actually usable for embodied control? This dimension focuses on whether predicted futures preserve the physical and action-relevant structure required for execution, including consistency with dynamics, responsiveness to action interventions, and recoverability into valid control signals. Rather than measuring overall prediction quality or end-task success, it probes the specific failure modes that often explain why visually plausible rollouts still fail in planning, policy evaluation, or execution.\nWorldSimBench (Qin et al.,2025) is representative of this direction. It combines perceptual evaluation with manipulative evaluation, asking not only whether generated videos look realistic, but also whether they remain sufficiently consistent with action and environment dynamics to support inverse-dynamics recovery and downstream control. WoW-World-Eval (Fan et al.,2026) provides a broader but closely related perspective. Although it spans perception, planning, prediction, execution, and generalization, it is especially relevant here because it introduces physical-law and execution-oriented criteria, including an IDM-based Turing Test for whether generated videos induce plausible and executable actions. Together, these benchmarks make clear that visual plausibility alone is insufficient: generated rollouts must also preserve physically grounded and operationally executable action consequences.\n25\net al., 2025a)评估指令遵循和物理对齐,考察生成的推演轨迹作为策略学习的合成经验是否有用,而不仅 仅是逼真。EVA‐Bench(Chi et al., 2025b)通过强调在视角、场景布局和运动分布变化下的长时程预测和 域外鲁棒性,补充了这一观点。总体而言,这些基准表明,强大的开环世界模型必须做的不仅仅是生成看似 合理的未来:它们必须保持动作锚定、物理合理,并且足够鲁棒以支持下游具身应用。\n7.1.2 闭环任务效用与策略评估\n开环基准评估世界模型能否生成以动作为条件的未来,而闭环基准则追问这些预测在交互式决策循环中是否 仍然有用。在此设定下,世界模型不是作为被动预测器来评估,而是作为环境模拟器、策略评估器或规划基 座来评估,直接影响随时间推移的动作选择。因此,关注点从预测合理性转向决策效用:模型是否保留了策 略排序、价值估计、规划以及最终任务成功所需的任务相关动态。这使得闭环评估更契合具身智能,因为一 旦智能体反复基于模型生成的未来采取行动,微小的建模误差可能累积并破坏控制。\n该类别下的近期基准在协议上各有不同,但共享同一原则:有用的具身世界模型必须支持下游决策,而不仅 仅是逼真的生成。WorldArena(Shang et al., 2026)明确体现了这一点,它不仅以感知标准评估世界模 型,还通过合成数据生成、策略评估和动作规划等功能性角色来评估,突显了视觉逼真度与具身效用之间的 差距。WorldEval(Li et al., 2025e)通过比较性策略评估将这一理念付诸实践,追问在学习到的世界模型 中的 rollout 是否保留了机器人策略与检查点的相对排序。WorldGym(Quevedo et al., 2025)进一步扩 展了这一设定,将学习到的模型视为用于蒙特卡洛评估的交互式环境,关注估计的策略价值和成功趋势是否 与真实世界一致。在这些工作中,排序一致性、价值保真度和决策可靠性成为比像素级精度更具信息量的标 准。\n这一评估的更严格版本将世界模型直接置于闭环规划流程中,并衡量具身任务的成功率。World‐in‐World (Zhang et al., 2025a)是这一设定的代表性工作:通过提供统一接口将异构世界模型集成到在线规划任 务中,它检验模型是否能在迭代预测与重新规划的背景下提升控制效果。这比开环推演评估更为困难,因为 它会暴露预测与动作随时间交互时产生的累积误差。总体而言,近期证据表明,视觉合理性仅是控制效用的 弱代理指标,而基于动作的一致性与可控性则是下游具身有用性更为可靠的指标。\n7.1.3 物理一致性、可控性与可执行性诊断\n开环基准评估预测质量,闭环基准评估下游效用,而诊断性基准则提出一个更有针对性的问题:生成推演的 哪些属性决定了它是否真正可用于具身控制?这一维度关注预测的未来是否保留了执行所需的物理结构和动 作相关结构,包括与动力学的 consistency、对动作干预的响应性以及恢复到有效控制信号的可恢复性。它 不衡量整体预测质量或最终任务成功率,而是探究那些常能解释为何视觉上合理的推演在规划、策略评估或 执行中仍然失败的具体失效模式。\nWorldSimBench(Qin 等,2025)是这一方向的代表性工作。它将感知评估与操作评估相结合,不仅考察 生成的视频是否看起来真实,还考察其是否与动作和环境动力学保持足够的一致性,以支持逆动力学恢复和 下游控制。WoW‐World‐Eval(Fan 等,2026)提供了一个更广泛但密切相关的视角。尽管它涵盖感知、规 划、预测、执行和泛化,但它在此处尤为相关,因为它引入了物理定律导向和执行导向的评估标准,包括基 于 IDM 的图灵测试,用于判断生成的视频是否能诱导出合理且可执行的动作。总之,这些基准清晰地表明, 仅凭视觉合理性是不够的:生成的 rollout 还必须保留具有物理依据且可操作执行的动作后果。\n25\n\n\n Table 3Core attributes of representative datasets/resources for embodied world model training. Benchmark-only resources discussed in the evaluation section are excluded.\nName Year Source X-Emb. Act. Obs./3D Lang. M/C\nRoVid-X (Deng et al.,2026) 2026 Real/Robot video – – –✓✗ Open X-Embodiment (OXE)\n(O’Neill et al.,2024) 2024 Real✓✓– – – DROID (Khazatsky et al.,2024) 2024 Real✗✓–✓– BridgeData V2 (Walke et al.,2023) 2023 Real✗✓–✓– AgiBot World (Bu et al.,2025a) 2025 Real –✓–✓– Galaxea Open-World Datase\n(Jiang et al.,2025a) 2025 Real –✓–✓– Humanoid Everyday (Zhao et al.,2025) 2025 Real –✓✓✓✓ RoboMIND 2.0 (Hou et al.,2025) 2025 Real+Sim✓✓–✓✓ FastUMI-100K (Liu et al.,2025b) 2025 Real –✓✓✓– BRMData (Zhang et al.,2024) 2024 Real –✓✓– – UMI (Chi et al.,2024) 2024 Real –✓– –✗ MV-UMI (Rayyan et al.,2025) 2025 Real✓✓✓–✗ ActiveUMI (Zeng et al.,2025) 2025 Real –✓✓–✗ TWIST2 (Ze et al.,2025) 2025 Real –✓–✗✗ DexWild (Tao et al.,2025) 2025 Human+Robot✓– –✗✗ EgoMimic (Kareer et al.,2025) 2025 Human+Robot – –✓✗✗ PHSD / In-N-On (Cai et al.,2025) 2025 Human ego✗– –✗✗ UniHand (Luo et al.,2025) 2025 Human video – – –✓✗ UniHand 2.0 (Luo et al.,2026) 2026 Human+Robot+VLM✓✓–✓✗ Hoi! (Engelbracht et al.,2025) 2025 Human+Robot✓✓✓✗✓ FreeTacMan (Wu et al.,2025) 2025 Ro